## Section 1 & 2: Research Framing, Epistemological Standards, and 15-Day Constraints

Building an agricultural decision-support system within a 15-day hackathon window (maximum 20 days) with a four-student engineering team requires separating what is scientifically sound and demonstrable from what is promotional vaporware. Across Indian and global agritech, hundreds of student and startup projects fail because they build a generic "AI Farming Assistant" wrapping a basic weather widget, a static NPK table downloaded from Kaggle, and an uncalibrated LLM chatbot. Such systems neither predict actual plot-level yield risk nor optimize physical resource allocation.

To maintain strict scientific rigor, every claim in this report is categorized under five epistemological labels:

1. **Verified Fact:** Directly documented in official government portals, peer-reviewed studies, or API specifications (e.g., [SoilGrids 2.0](https://soil.copernicus.org/articles/7/217/2021/) provides 250 m spatial resolution across six standard soil depth intervals).
2. **Research Finding:** Empirical results demonstrated under controlled academic experimental setups (e.g., fusing [Copernicus Sentinel-1 SAR and Sentinel-2 optical data](https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10106049.2026.2690363) yields a 5–8% classification and yield modeling gain in cloud-prone tropical regions).
3. **Product Marketing Claim:** Figures self-reported by commercial vendors without independent peer-reviewed replication (e.g., commercial platforms claiming "up to 30% yield increases" across broad user cohorts).
4. **Explicit Assumption:** Transparent parameter estimates used for financial and operational modeling (e.g., assuming a standard smallholder parcel size of 1.0 to 2.0 hectares in Telangana/Andhra Pradesh and local borewell pumping or urea/DAP input costs).
5. **Proposed Hypothesis:** Testable engineering propositions for our hackathon prototype (e.g., that combining [Conformal Quantile Regression](https://csridjournal.potensi-utama.org/index.php/CSRIDjournal/en/article/download/398/130/2214) on fused SAR-optical-weather features with [FAO-33/56 water-yield response](https://www.fao.org/3/i2800e/i2800e.pdf) and [ICAR STCR nutrient equations](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/plant-science/articles/10.3389/fpls.2024.1439523/full) produces actionable, budget-constrained water and fertilizer savings within a 15-day build).

## Section 3: Foundational Investigation — Answering the 10 Core Questions

### 1. What are the actual problems farmers currently experience?
Indian smallholder and marginal farmers—who operate over 85% of landholdings in India ([NAAS Policy Paper 101](https://naas.org.in/Policy%20Papers/Policy%20101.pdf))—face compounding operational blind spots during the active cropping cycle. Rather than lacking generic textbook knowledge on how to grow paddy, cotton, maize, or groundnut, farmers struggle with **in-season timing and dosage decisions under weather volatility**: knowing whether a mid-season dry spell during flowering requires immediate supplemental irrigation to prevent irreversible grain-filling collapse, or whether applying another bag of top-dress urea after heavy rainfall will simply leach into groundwater or volatilize without increasing yield ([OpenAccessPub Agronomy Research](https://openaccesspub.org/agronomy-research/article/indian-agriculture-needs-a-strategic-shift-for-improving-fertilizer-response-and-overcome-sluggish-foodgrain-production-1752)).

### 2. Which of these problems cause significant financial losses?
Financial losses concentrate in two areas: **input resource waste (fertilizer and water)** and **unmitigated mid-season yield shock**. Post-harvest losses alone cost Indian agriculture ₹1.53 trillion (USD 18.5 billion) annually according to the NABCONS study cited by [ICRIER Policy Brief 20](https://icrier.org/pdf/Policy_Brief_20.pdf), while pre-harvest on-farm losses are driven by declining fertilizer response ratios and moisture stress at critical phenological stages ([ResearchGate Marathwada Yield Loss Study](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/341479156_On_the_role_of_rainfall_deficits_and_cropping_choices_in_loss_of_agricultural_yield_in_Marathwada_India)). National dashboard data from the [Government of India Soil Health Card Portal](https://soilhealth.dac.gov.in/nutrient-dashboard) confirms that roughly 79% of tested Indian soil samples are low in available Nitrogen ($N$), while Phosphorus ($P$) and Potassium ($K$) exhibit severe regional imbalances. Because urea is heavily subsidized relative to phosphatic and potassic fertilizers, farmers routinely over-apply urea while under-applying micronutrients and potassium, driving down grain-to-nutrient conversion efficiency and inflating input expenditure by ₹4,000–₹9,000 per hectare per season without proportional yield gain ([Frontiers in Plant Science STCR Study](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/plant-science/articles/10.3389/fpls.2024.1439523/full)).

### 3. Where do existing agricultural technologies fail?
Existing agricultural technologies fail at the **translation layer between prediction and resource optimization**, and fail operationally during the **Indian Kharif monsoon (June–October)**. First, most satellite-based monitoring apps rely exclusively on optical vegetation indices such as NDVI from Sentinel-2 or Landsat. During Kharif, persistent cloud cover across peninsular and deltaic India renders optical satellite imagery unusable for weeks at a time; a recent [Frontiers in Sustainable Food Systems (2026) study in the Cauvery Delta](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/sustainable-food-systems/articles/10.3389/fsufs.2026.1875985/full) demonstrated that standalone optical NDVI achieved only 51.6–55.8% accuracy under block cross-validation—worse than a majority-class baseline—until fused with cloud-penetrating [Sentinel-1 C-band SAR backscatter](https://sentiwiki.copernicus.eu/web/s1-applications). Second, existing yield models output a single deterministic number (e.g., "Predicted Yield: 4.2 tonnes/ha") at the end of the season, offering zero uncertainty bounds and zero simulation of how changing irrigation or fertilizer split-doses *today* will shift the final yield and net profit.

### 4. Which problems are inadequately addressed by available commercial products?
Commercial platforms leave a massive gap between **hardware-heavy precision horticulture** and **macro-level institutional analytics**:
- IoT-based platforms like [Fasal (Wolkus Technology)](https://www.fasal.co/) provide accurate microclimate and soil moisture scheduling, but require installing physical on-farm sensor nodes ([Fasal Main Unit](https://www.fasal.co/main-unit) and [Soil Moisture Unit 6.0](https://www.fasal.co/soil-moisture-unit)) costing tens of thousands of rupees, restricting adoption to high-value horticulture orchards (grapes, pomegranates, bananas) rather than 1–2 hectare cereal, pulse, or oilseed farmers.
- B2B enterprise platforms like [Cropin SmartRisk/SmartFarm](https://www.cropin.com/) and government platforms like [Krishi-DSS](https://krishi-dss.gov.in/) and [FASAL (MNCFC)](https://www.ncfc.gov.in/fasal.html) serve state departments, banks, insurers, and procurement corporations at district or Gram Panchayat scales ([PIB Release 2297646](https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=2297646&reg=3&lang=1)), not individual farmers seeking plot-specific input optimization.
- Consumer apps like [Plantix](https://akta.pro/company-profiles/0004e4s/plantix) focus on reactive leaf-photo disease diagnosis *after* visible symptoms appear ([Khetiyaar 2026 Comparison](https://www.khetiyaar.com/en/blog/best-ai-farming-apps-india)), while input marketplaces like AgroStar and [DeHaat](https://www.khetiyaar.com/en/blog/best-ai-farming-apps-india) tie advisory to retail input sales.

### 5. Which problems have research demonstrating that existing solutions are unreliable or insufficient?
Peer-reviewed literature documents three critical failure modes in current crop yield and advisory systems:
1. **Optical Satellite Blindness in Monsoon Cropping:** As proven in the [2026 Cauvery Delta Sentinel-1/2 study](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/sustainable-food-systems/articles/10.3389/fsufs.2026.1875985/full) and the [2026 PRISMA Systematic Review of 111 Sentinel studies](https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10106049.2026.2690363), optical-only crop monitoring fails during cloud-occluded vegetative and reproductive phases unless paired with Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) time-series features ($VV$, $VH$, and Radar Vegetation Index $RVI$).
2. **Blanket State Fertilizer Recommendations vs. Targeted Yield Equations:** Field trials published by ICAR researchers in [Frontiers in Plant Science (2024)](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/plant-science/articles/10.3389/fpls.2024.1439523/full) and [PLOS ONE (2026)](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0341724) prove that general recommended doses (GRD) consistently underperform Soil Test Crop Response (STCR) targeted-yield equations by 8.88% (soybean) to 24.53% (aerobic rice) and 25.85% (sunflower), while wasting fertilizer on non-responsive soils.
3. **Uncalibrated Point Predictions & Spatial Data Leakage:** Academic reviews ([Open-Source Indian Agriculture Datasets Guide 2026](https://aigrants.in/topics/open-source-indian-agriculture-datasets-for-machine-learning)) highlight that most published ML yield models suffer from random train-test split leakage (mixing the same district or weather year across train and test sets) and fail to provide calibrated confidence intervals ([CSRID 2026 CatBoost/XGBoost Conformal Study](https://csridjournal.potensi-utama.org/index.php/CSRIDjournal/en/article/download/398/130/2214)).

### 6. Which problems can realistically be addressed using AI?
Machine learning—specifically gradient-boosted decision trees ([CatBoost and XGBoost](https://academicresearchlibrary.com/abstract-detail/4734)) combined with distribution-free **Conformal Prediction**—excels at learning non-linear interactions across heterogeneous tabular and time-series features: multi-stage weather anomalies (Growing Degree Days, Vapor Pressure Deficit, cumulative rainfall deficits), soil physical/chemical baselines ([SoilGrids 250m](https://isric.org/explore/soilgrids) + [Soil Health Card](https://soilhealth.dac.gov.in/) inputs), and cloud-free satellite phenology curves (Sentinel-2 NDRE/NDVI fused with Sentinel-1 $VH/VV$ backscatter). When this ML yield response surface is coupled with deterministic biophysical constraints ([FAO-56 dual crop coefficient water balance](https://www.fao.org/3/i2800e/i2800e.pdf) and [ICAR STCR nutrient equations](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/plant-science/articles/10.3389/fpls.2024.1439523/full)), the system can run **counterfactual "what-if" interventions**—predicting the exact marginal yield gain ($\Delta Y$, in kg/ha) and marginal net return ($\Delta ₹$/ha) of applying a specific irrigation depth (mm) or split-nitrogen dose (kg/ha) at the current crop growth stage.

### 7. Which of these problems can be demonstrated within 15 days?
Within 15 days, a four-person student team cannot deploy physical IoT sensors across 500 farms or collect multi-year proprietary plot-cutting experiments from scratch. However, the team **can** build and validate a working end-to-end system for **3–4 major regional crops (e.g., Rice/Paddy, Maize, Cotton, and Groundnut across Telangana and Andhra Pradesh)** that:
1. Ingests historical district-level yield series ([ICRISAT DLD / Ministry of Agriculture APS](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/sustainable-food-systems/articles/10.3389/fsufs.2024.1357201/full)) and sub-district satellite/weather features to train a temporally validated **Conformal CatBoost/XGBoost Yield & Stress Model**.
2. Fetches live plot-level coordinates (latitude/longitude) to pull real-time daily agroclimatology from [NASA POWER API](https://packages.ropensci.org/nasapower) and 16-day forecasts/soil moisture from [Open-Meteo API](https://open-meteo.com/en/docs), baseline soil properties from [ISRIC SoilGrids 2.0](https://isric.org/explore/soilgrids) (with optional farmer override from a [Soil Health Card](https://soilhealth.dac.gov.in/)), and 10m optical/SAR vegetation signatures via [Google Earth Engine Python API](https://developers.google.com/earth-engine/tutorials/community/intro-to-python-api) or [Copernicus Data Space Sentinel Hub API](https://dataspace.copernicus.eu/analyse/apis/sentinel-hub).
3. Runs a live **Counterfactual Resource Optimizer** that shows the judges: *"If you do nothing over the next 10 days, expected yield is $3,820 \pm 310\text{ kg/ha}$. If you apply $35\text{ mm}$ irrigation in 3 days + $22\text{ kg/ha}$ urea split-dose (skipping phosphorus), expected yield rises to $4,390 \pm 260\text{ kg/ha}$, saving $25\text{ mm}$ of water and ₹1,850/ha in unnecessary fertilizer for a net gain of +₹11,400/ha."*

### 8. What information and datasets are required?
The pipeline requires five accessible, verified open datasets/APIs:
- **Historical Crop Yield Labels:** [Directorate of Economics and Statistics (DES) / data.gov.in](https://aigrants.in/topics/open-source-indian-agriculture-datasets-for-machine-learning) and [ICRISAT District Level Database (DLD)](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/sustainable-food-systems/articles/10.3389/fsufs.2024.1357201/full) covering multi-decade district-wise seasonal area, production, and yield (tonnes/ha) across Indian states.
- **Daily Historical & Real-Time Agroclimatology:** [NASA POWER Agroclimatology API](https://packages.ropensci.org/nasapower) (`T2M_MAX`, `T2M_MIN`, `PRECTOTCORR`, `ALLSKY_SFC_SW_DWN`, `RH2M`) and [IMD Gridded Data via `imdlib`](https://imdlib.readthedocs.io/en/latest/), paired with [Open-Meteo Forecast API](https://open-meteo.com/en/docs) for 14-day ahead precipitation, reference evapotranspiration ($ET_0$), and multi-depth volumetric soil moisture.
- **All-Weather Satellite Remote Sensing:** [Copernicus Sentinel-2 L2A (10m optical NDVI, NDRE, NDWI)](https://dataspace.copernicus.eu/data-collections/copernicus-sentinel-missions/sentinel-2) and [Copernicus Sentinel-1 GRD (10m C-band SAR $VV$, $VH$ backscatter)](https://dataspace.copernicus.eu/data-collections/copernicus-sentinel-missions/sentinel-1) accessed via [Google Earth Engine](https://earthengine.google.com/) or [Copernicus Data Space Ecosystem](https://dataspace.copernicus.eu/).
- **Soil Physical & Chemical Baseline:** [ISRIC SoilGrids 2.0 (250m resolution)](https://soil.copernicus.org/articles/7/217/2021/) for clay, silt, sand, bulk density, pH, soil organic carbon (SOC), cation exchange capacity (CEC), and total nitrogen, supplemented by district nutrient distribution baselines from the [Soil Health Card Portal](https://soilhealth.dac.gov.in/nutrient-dashboard).
- **Agronomic Response Parameters:** [FAO Irrigation and Drainage Paper No. 56 & No. 33](https://www.fao.org/3/i2800e/i2800e.pdf) stage-wise crop coefficients ($K_c$) and yield response factors ($K_y$), plus [ICAR Soil Test Crop Response (STCR)](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/plant-science/articles/10.3389/fpls.2024.1439523/full) targeted yield coefficients ($NR$, $CS$, $CF$).

### 9. What would make our solution substantially different from existing competitors?
Existing tools either give **macro-level yield forecasts with zero farmer actionability** ([FASAL](https://www.ncfc.gov.in/fasal.html), [YES-TECH](https://www.pmfby.gov.in/pdf/YES_TECH_Manual_2023.pdf), [Krishi-DSS](https://krishi-dss.gov.in/)) or provide **static crop calendars / hardware-dependent alerts** ([Fasal IoT](https://www.fasal.co/), [DeHaat](https://www.khetiyaar.com/en/blog/best-ai-farming-apps-india)). Our solution introduces three concrete technical differentiators:
1. **Closed-Loop Counterfactual Yield-to-Input Optimization:** Instead of treating yield prediction and input advisory as separate modules, our system uses the yield prediction model + biophysical [FAO-56](https://www.fao.org/3/i2800e/i2800e.pdf)/[STCR](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/plant-science/articles/10.3389/fpls.2024.1439523/full) equations as an objective function to rank the **Minimum Effective Intervention** (exact irrigation mm and fertilizer kg/ha under the farmer's cash/water budget) against a "Do-Nothing" baseline.
2. **All-Weather SAR + Optical Fusion with Graceful Missing-Data Degradation:** When monsoon clouds block Sentinel-2 optical imagery, the pipeline automatically switches to Sentinel-1 C-band SAR radar vegetation indices ($VH/VV$) and soil water balance tracking ([Tandfonline 2026 SAR-Optical Fusion Review](https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10106049.2026.2690363)), and works even if the farmer has no physical Soil Health Card by falling back to [SoilGrids 250m](https://isric.org/explore/soilgrids) priors with widened uncertainty bands.
3. **Calibrated Conformal Prediction Intervals:** Every yield forecast and ROI recommendation carries a mathematically guaranteed $80\% / 90\%$ confidence interval ([Conformal Quantile Regression](https://csridjournal.potensi-utama.org/index.php/CSRIDjournal/en/article/download/398/130/2214)), preventing false precision when input data is noisy or coarse.

### 10. Why would an actual farmer use this system instead of an existing application?
A smallholder farmer does not care about an NDVI heatmap or a raw $R^2$ score; a farmer cares about **saving cash on unnecessary fertilizer bags, conserving limited borewell water during dry spells, and protecting harvest revenue**. Unlike [Fasal](https://www.fasal.co/), our system requires **₹0 in on-field IoT hardware**. Unlike [Plantix](https://akta.pro/company-profiles/0004e4s/plantix), which waits until leaves are visibly damaged by pests or nutrient starvation, our system flags **sub-visual water and nitrogen deficits 10–15 days earlier** via red-edge/SAR satellite anomalies and weather water-balance deficits, and translates them into local units (bags of Urea/DAP per acre, hours of pump irrigation, and net ₹ ROI) accessible via a lightweight offline-capable PWA or WhatsApp/SMS summary.

