### 5.3 Critical Originality and Prior-Art Disclosure

In strict compliance with Section 5's originality mandate: **we explicitly state that satellite-based crop yield prediction and satellite-based irrigation/soil advisories are NOT novel on their own.** Government programs ([FASAL](https://www.ncfc.gov.in/fasal.html), [YES-TECH](https://www.pmfby.gov.in/pdf/YES_TECH_Manual_2023.pdf)), enterprise platforms ([Cropin](https://www.cropin.com/)), and startups ([Sat2Farm](https://satyukt.com/sat2farm.html)) already compute NDVI/SAR vegetation indices and estimate yield or soil moisture.

Where existing solutions stop—and where our narrow, defensible innovation lies—is the **Integration Gap between Uncertainty-Calibrated Yield Prediction and Prescriptive, Budget-Constrained Resource Optimization**:
1. Existing tools treat yield prediction as a **passive reporting metric** (for governments/insurers) and treat crop advisory as **static calendar rules** ("Apply 50 kg Urea at Day 30").
2. None of the audited smallholder tools expose a **closed-loop counterfactual simulator** that combines:
   - **All-weather SAR-Optical-Weather Conformal Yield Prediction** ($P_{10}, P_{50}, P_{90}$ via CatBoost/XGBoost),
   - **Biophysical Stage Sensitivity Constraints** ([FAO-33/56](https://www.fao.org/3/i2800e/i2800e.pdf) $K_c$ and $K_y$ water-yield response and [ICAR STCR](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/plant-science/articles/10.3389/fpls.2024.1439523/full) targeted nutrient equations), and
   - **Downside-Safe Economic Optimization** (allocating a farmer's limited water hours and cash budget to maximize net return $\Delta ₹/\text{ha}$ while guaranteeing Value-Cost Ratio $VCR \ge 2.0$ even at the $P_{20}$ lower confidence bound).

## Section 6: Generation of 8 Distinct Innovative Project Concepts

To ensure breadth of exploration before convergence, eight distinct project concepts addressing different facets of the problem statement were engineered and evaluated across all 10 required dimensions.

### Concept 1: KharifShield (CropBudge-AI) — Uncertainty-Aware SAR-Optical Yield & Counterfactual Water/NPK ROI Optimizer
1. **Project Identity:**
   - **Name:** *KharifShield (CropBudge-AI)*
   - **One-Line Description:** An all-weather SAR-optical yield predictor and counterfactual decision optimizer that converts calibrated yield confidence intervals into stage-specific, budget-constrained irrigation and fertilizer actions.
   - **Main Objective:** Maximize net farm profit ($\text{₹/ha}$) and minimize water/fertilizer waste by simulating how today's irrigation and split-NPK choices shift final harvest yield distributions.
   - **Primary Target User:** Small and medium irrigated/semi-irrigated field-crop farmers (1–5 ha) growing paddy, maize, cotton, and groundnut, as well as FPO (Farmer Producer Organization) extension officers.
2. **Real-World Problem:** Smallholders over-apply urea and flood-irrigate out of fear of yield loss, yet suffer 15–30% yield drops during mid-season dry spells because optical satellite advisories go blind during monsoon clouds and static advisories ignore the farmer's cash/water constraints.
3. **Proposed Solution & User Journey:** The farmer drops a GPS pin on their plot, selects their crop (e.g., Kharif Paddy or Maize) and sowing date, and optionally enters their Soil Health Card values or current cash/water budget. The backend automatically fetches daily weather ([NASA POWER](https://packages.ropensci.org/nasapower) + [Open-Meteo](https://open-meteo.com/en/docs)), soil baselines ([SoilGrids 250m](https://isric.org/explore/soilgrids)), and 10m [Sentinel-2 optical + Sentinel-1 SAR](https://dataspace.copernicus.eu/) vegetation curves. A Conformal CatBoost model predicts baseline yield ($P_{10}, P_{50}, P_{90}$), and a coupled [FAO-56](https://www.fao.org/3/i2800e/i2800e.pdf) + [ICAR-STCR](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/plant-science/articles/10.3389/fpls.2024.1439523/full) optimizer outputs a ranked action card comparing "Do Nothing" vs. "Optimal Intervention" (exact mm of water, kg of Urea/DAP/MOP, cost in ₹, water saved, and net ₹ ROI).
4. **Innovation:** Bridges the gap between passive satellite monitoring ([Sat2Farm](https://satyukt.com/sat2farm.html), [FASAL](https://www.ncfc.gov.in/fasal.html)) and expensive IoT hardware ([Fasal](https://www.fasal.co/)) by combining cloud-penetrating SAR-optical fusion, Conformal Quantile Regression, and counterfactual biophysical optimization in a zero-hardware web app.
5. **AI Involvement:**
   - *Machine Learning:* CatBoost/XGBoost with Conformal Quantile Regression (`Mapie` / quantile loss) for calibrated $P_{10}/P_{50}/P_{90}$ yield prediction and SHAP for feature attribution.
   - *Time-Series & Satellite Processing:* Phenological stage alignment (GDD-indexed) of Sentinel-2 NDRE/NDVI and Sentinel-1 $VH/VV$ radar backscatter time series.
   - *Optimization Algorithms:* Constrained grid/linear programming over [FAO-33/56](https://www.fao.org/3/i2800e/i2800e.pdf) ($K_y$) and [ICAR STCR](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/plant-science/articles/10.3389/fpls.2024.1439523/full) equations maximizing net margin subject to water/budget caps. (No unnecessary LLMs or heavy 3D CNNs).
6. **Data Requirements:** [ICRISAT / data.gov.in](https://aigrants.in/topics/open-source-indian-agriculture-datasets-for-machine-learning) district yield history, [NASA POWER](https://packages.ropensci.org/nasapower) daily agroclimatology, [Open-Meteo](https://open-meteo.com/en/docs) 14-day forecasts & soil moisture, [ISRIC SoilGrids 2.0](https://isric.org/explore/soilgrids), and [Sentinel-1/2 via GEE/CDSE](https://earthengine.google.com/).
7. **Technical Feasibility (15 Days):** Highly feasible. Tabular + pre-aggregated GEE satellite time series train in <2 minutes on a laptop CPU/GPU ([ASUS VivoBook RTX 3050 class hardware](https://academicresearchlibrary.com/abstract-detail/4734)); deterministic FAO-56/STCR optimization runs in <50 milliseconds.
8. **User Benefits:** Under explicit assumptions (1 ha irrigated maize/paddy plot, ₹266/bag subsidized urea, ₹1,350/bag DAP, and limited borewell pumping hours), eliminating 1 redundant urea split/DAP overdose and skipping 2 non-critical irrigations during low-$K_y$ vegetative stages saves **20–30% irrigation water** and **₹2,500–₹4,500/ha in fertilizer**, while protecting **300–600 kg/ha (₹6,500–₹13,000/ha)** of yield during flowering moisture stress.
9. **Limitations:** Historical training labels from public Indian sources are predominantly district-level; plot-level yield variations rely on semi-physical satellite/soil downscaling modifiers rather than thousands of proprietary plot-level CCE labels.
10. **Demonstration Potential:** Live interactive dashboard where judges click any farm plot in Telangana/India, inspect real Sentinel-1 SAR vs. Sentinel-2 optical curves through monsoon clouds, see the $P_{10}$–$P_{90}$ yield fan chart, and drag "Available Water" and "Fertilizer Budget (₹)" sliders to watch the optimal action plan and net profit update in real time.

### Concept 2: StageDrop — Crop-Stage Evapotranspiration Deficit & Borewell Water-Budget Scheduler
1. **Project Identity:**
   - **Name:** *StageDrop*
   - **One-Line Description:** A virtual root-zone soil moisture twin and deficit-irrigation scheduler that maximizes crop yield per cubic meter of scarce borewell water.
   - **Main Objective:** Prevent catastrophic mid-season crop failure when groundwater drops by dynamically reallocating limited irrigation water to high-sensitivity phenological stages ($K_y > 1.0$).
   - **Primary Target User:** Borewell-dependent smallholder farmers in semi-arid districts (Telangana, Marathwada, Rayalaseema, North Karnataka).
2. **Real-World Problem:** Farmers irrigate heavily during early vegetative growth when water is plentiful, depleting their borewell or farm pond right before flowering and grain filling—when moisture stress causes a $1.25\times$ to $1.58\times$ proportional yield collapse ([Subsurface Drip $K_y$ Study 2022](https://era.dpi.qld.gov.au/id/eprint/921/)).
3. **Proposed Solution & User Journey:** Farmer enters crop, sowing date, pump discharge rate (or hours of electricity available per week), and soil texture. StageDrop tracks daily root-zone water balance using [Open-Meteo](https://open-meteo.com/en/docs) $ET_0$, rainfall, and Sentinel-2 NDVI-derived basal crop coefficients ($K_{cb}$), solving a dynamic program over the remaining season to schedule exact pump hours per week.
4. **Innovation:** Replaces ₹35,000 [Fasal IoT soil probes](https://www.fasal.co/soil-moisture-unit) with a satellite-calibrated [FAO-56 dual crop coefficient water balance](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/392694920_Cowpea_evapotranspiration_modeling_using_the_FAO-56_dual_crop_coefficient_approach_for_climate-resilient_agriculture_under_deficit_irrigation_conditions) and multi-stage deficit irrigation optimizer.
5. **AI Involvement:** Gradient-boosted regression to estimate local actual crop coefficient ($K_c$) from Sentinel-2 NDVI/NDWI, paired with dynamic programming over the [FAO-33 multiplicative yield response function](https://www.fao.org/3/i2800e/i2800e.pdf).
6. **Data Requirements:** [Open-Meteo API](https://open-meteo.com/en/docs), [NASA POWER](https://packages.ropensci.org/nasapower), [SoilGrids 250m](https://isric.org/explore/soilgrids) (water retention capacity from clay/sand/SOC pedotransfer functions), and [FAO-56](https://www.fao.org/3/i2800e/i2800e.pdf) crop tables.
7. **Technical Feasibility (15 Days):** Very high; requires lightweight API integration and clean mathematical optimization.
8. **User Benefits:** Saves 25–35% seasonal pumping water while preserving >92% of maximum potential yield under Regulated Deficit Irrigation (RDI).
9. **Limitations:** Focuses exclusively on water-yield dynamics; does not address fertilizer optimization or pest/nutrient interactions on its own.
10. **Demonstration Potential:** Side-by-side seasonal simulation comparing "Traditional Fixed-Interval Irrigation" (running out of water at Day 75, losing 38% yield) vs. "StageDrop Regulated Deficit Schedule" (preserving flowering moisture, losing only 4% yield with 30% less water).

### Concept 3: NutriTarget-STCR — Satellite-Calibrated Soil Test Crop Response & Split-Dose Optimizer
1. **Project Identity:**
   - **Name:** *NutriTarget-STCR*
   - **One-Line Description:** An AI-calibrated fertilizer prescription engine that pairs OCR-scanned Soil Health Cards (or satellite soil priors) with in-season canopy chlorophyll sensing to optimize NPK split doses.
   - **Main Objective:** Eliminate urea over-application and correct NPK imbalances by computing targeted-yield fertilizer prescriptions adjusted for live mid-season crop vigor.
   - **Primary Target User:** Irrigated cereal, cotton, and vegetable farmers spending ₹8,000–₹20,000/ha on chemical fertilizers.
2. **Real-World Problem:** Static [Soil Health Card](https://soilhealth.dac.gov.in/) recommendations are ignored because they are hard to read and do not adapt when mid-season weather alters the realistic yield target or leaches basal nitrogen.
3. **Proposed Solution & User Journey:** Farmer snaps a photo of their printed Soil Health Card (or skips it to use [SoilGrids 2.0](https://isric.org/explore/soilgrids) + [SHC district priors](https://soilhealth.dac.gov.in/nutrient-dashboard)). Before each split top-dressing (e.g., 30 and 55 days after sowing), NutriTarget checks Sentinel-2 Red-Edge Chlorophyll Index ($CI_{\text{red-edge}}$) and weather history to compute the realistic yield ceiling ($T$, in q/ha) and runs [ICAR STCR equations](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/plant-science/articles/10.3389/fpls.2024.1439523/full) to prescribe exact kg of Urea, DAP, and MOP.
4. **Innovation:** Converts static government STCR equations into a **dynamic, in-season closed loop** driven by satellite red-edge chlorophyll sensing and ML yield ceiling estimation.
5. **AI Involvement:** OCR/parsing for Soil Health Cards, LightGBM/CatBoost for mid-season yield target ($T$) prediction, and constrained optimization for cheapest local fertilizer blend (Urea vs. DAP vs. NPK 10-26-26 vs. SSP+MOP).
6. **Data Requirements:** [ICAR AICRP-STCR](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/plant-science/articles/10.3389/fpls.2024.1439523/full) crop equations, [SoilGrids 2.0](https://isric.org/explore/soilgrids), [Soil Health Card Portal](https://soilhealth.dac.gov.in/nutrient-dashboard), and Sentinel-2 Red-Edge bands.
7. **Technical Feasibility (15 Days):** High; STCR equations are well-published in ICAR literature and linear blend optimization (`scipy.optimize.linprog`) is exact and instantaneous.
8. **User Benefits:** Empirical ICAR trials show STCR-IPNS increases yield by 8.9–25.8% over blanket doses while maintaining Value-Cost Ratio ($VCR > 2.5$) ([Frontiers in Plant Science 2024](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/plant-science/articles/10.3389/fpls.2024.1439523/full)).
9. **Limitations:** Optical red-edge bands still suffer during heavy Kharif cloud cover unless paired with SAR (as in Concept 1), and addresses nutrition more than irrigation.
10. **Demonstration Potential:** Live fertilizer cost-minimizer and yield-response curve showing how adjusting target yield $T$ based on mid-season satellite vigor saves ₹3,200/ha on unabsorbed fertilizer.

### Concept 4: SowWindow-AI — Probabilistic Monsoon-Onset & False-Start Risk Advisor for Rainfed Drylands
1. **Project Identity:**
   - **Name:** *SowWindow-AI*
   - **One-Line Description:** A probabilistic dryland sowing-date and variety-duration selector that prevents seed and fertilizer loss from false monsoon onsets.
   - **Main Objective:** Predict the probability of a post-sowing 14-day dry spell ("false onset") and recommend whether to sow immediately, wait 7 days, or switch to a short-duration drought-tolerant cultivar.
   - **Primary Target User:** Rainfed cotton, soybean, groundnut, and pulse farmers in semi-arid India.
2. **Real-World Problem:** Early pre-monsoon showers trick farmers into sowing; a subsequent 18-day dry spell kills germinating seeds, forcing costly re-sowing and yield loss ([ResearchGate Marathwada Study 2026](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/341479156_On_the_role_of_rainfall_deficits_and_cropping_choices_in_loss_of_agricultural_yield_in_Marathwada_India)).
3. **Proposed Solution & User Journey:** During May–July, the farmer checks their village's 14-day Germination Survival Score. The model combines 40-year historical IMD onset statistics ([`imdlib`](https://imdlib.readthedocs.io/en/latest/)), current multi-depth soil moisture ([Open-Meteo](https://open-meteo.com/en/docs)), and 16-day ensemble rainfall forecasts to simulate expected yield across three choices: *Sow Now (140-day variety)* vs. *Sow in 10 Days (120-day variety)* vs. *Switch Crop (e.g., Maize to Red Gram/Millet)*.
4. **Innovation:** Directly links pre-sowing meteorological risk to full-season yield prediction and cultivar-duration switching.
5. **AI Involvement:** Calibrated gradient-boosted classification for 14-day soil moisture survival probability + seasonal yield regression under shifted sowing dates.
6. **Data Requirements:** [IMD Gridded Rainfall/Temp (`imdlib`)](https://imdlib.readthedocs.io/en/latest/), [NASA POWER](https://packages.ropensci.org/nasapower), [Open-Meteo Ensemble API](https://open-meteo.com/en/docs), and [ICRISAT DLD](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/sustainable-food-systems/articles/10.3389/fsufs.2024.1357201/full).
7. **Technical Feasibility (15 Days):** High for historical backtesting, though live demonstration in November (Rabi season) relies on historical Kharif replay.
8. **User Benefits:** Prevents ₹5,000–₹8,000/ha re-sowing losses and protects 15–25% of seasonal yield.
9. **Limitations:** Only used once per season (at sowing time), offering less in-season resource optimization once the crop is already standing.
10. **Demonstration Potential:** Interactive historical replay of the 2023/2025 delayed monsoon in Telangana/Marathwada showing how SowWindow-AI would have saved a cotton/soybean farmer from a false-start wipeout.

### Concept 5: MissingModality-Net — Graceful-Degradation Tabular Ensemble for Sensorless & Zero-Soil-Card Plots
1. **Project Identity:**
   - **Name:** *MissingModality-Net*
   - **One-Line Description:** A fault-tolerant yield and advisory engine engineered via modality dropout to work seamlessly even when soil tests, optical satellites, or local weather stations are missing.
   - **Main Objective:** Guarantee reliable yield predictions and calibrated uncertainty intervals under real-world rural data scarcity.
   - **Primary Target User:** Marginal farmers in data-sparse districts with zero soil testing and intermittent connectivity.
2. **Real-World Problem:** Most academic ML models assume a complete feature vector; in production, a farmer lacks lab N/P/K values, Sentinel-2 is cloudy, or a local weather station is offline, causing standard pipelines to fail or hallucinate via naive mean imputation.
3. **Proposed Solution & User Journey:** Uses a sub-model ensemble / modality-dropout architecture (CatBoost native missing-value routing + Conformal Prediction) that dynamically adjusts its prediction interval width based on which data tiers are available, showing the user exactly how much narrower their yield forecast becomes if they add a 30-second field observation (e.g., crop height or leaf color chart score).
4. **Innovation:** Quantifies the **Value of Information (VoI)**—showing the farmer how providing one extra input (like a Soil Health Card or leaf color check) tightens their recommendation accuracy.
5. **AI Involvement:** CatBoost with structured modality dropout during training + Split Conformal Prediction stratified by modality completeness.
6. **Data Requirements:** Same open stack ([ICRISAT](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/sustainable-food-systems/articles/10.3389/fsufs.2024.1357201/full), [NASA POWER](https://packages.ropensci.org/nasapower), [SoilGrids](https://isric.org/explore/soilgrids), [Sentinel-1/2](https://dataspace.copernicus.eu/)).
7. **Technical Feasibility (15 Days):** Very high; can be integrated directly as a core architectural feature of our winning solution.
8. **User Benefits:** Zero onboarding friction—works on Day 1 with just GPS + Crop + Sowing Date.
9. **Limitations:** More of a **foundational ML engineering feature** than a standalone product narrative for hackathon judges unless paired with Concept 1's resource optimizer.
10. **Demonstration Potential:** Live "Modality Toggle" where judges turn off Optical Satellite or Soil Lab inputs and watch the system gracefully fall back to SAR/SoilGrids while widening its confidence interval honestly.

### Concept 6: MilletsPulse — Transfer-Learned Yield & Input Optimizer for Under-Researched Semi-Arid Crops
1. **Project Identity:**
   - **Name:** *MilletsPulse*
   - **One-Line Description:** A transfer-learning yield predictor and micro-irrigation/STCR advisor tailored specifically to climate-resilient millets (jowar, bajra, ragi), pulses (red gram, Bengal gram), and oilseeds (groundnut, mustard).
   - **Main Objective:** Bring precision yield forecasting and targeted nutrient/water optimization to dryland crops neglected by commercial agritech.
   - **Primary Target User:** Dryland smallholders and Millet FPOs in Telangana, Karnataka, AP, Maharashtra, and Rajasthan.
2. **Real-World Problem:** Commercial platforms focus on high-margin horticulture ([Fasal](https://www.fasal.co/)) or paddy/wheat ([YES-TECH](https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=2297646&reg=3&lang=1)), even though dryland farmers growing millets, pulses, and oilseeds experience the highest climate vulnerability and achieve 22–26% yield gains when given targeted STCR nutrition ([Frontiers in Plant Science 2024](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/plant-science/articles/10.3389/fpls.2024.1439523/full)).
3. **Proposed Solution & User Journey:** Pooled multi-crop tabular transfer learning (using hierarchical crop embeddings across cereal/pulse/oilseed families in [ICRISAT DLD](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/sustainable-food-systems/articles/10.3389/fsufs.2024.1357201/full)) combined with life-saving single-irrigation ("protective irrigation") scheduling at critical flowering stages.
4. **Innovation:** First hackathon-ready decision optimizer focused on protective deficit irrigation and STCR nutrition for India's dryland millets, pulses, and oilseeds.
5. **AI Involvement:** Multi-task / hierarchical CatBoost model sharing meteorological/soil representations across data-rich and data-sparse crops.
6. **Data Requirements:** [ICRISAT DLD](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/sustainable-food-systems/articles/10.3389/fsufs.2024.1357201/full), [ICAR Dryland STCR Equations](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/plant-science/articles/10.3389/fpls.2024.1439523/full), [NASA POWER](https://packages.ropensci.org/nasapower), and [Sentinel-1/2](https://dataspace.copernicus.eu/).
7. **Technical Feasibility (15 Days):** High; ICRISAT DLD explicitly includes sorghum, pearl millet, finger millet, chickpea, pigeonpea, and groundnut.
8. **User Benefits:** Boosts dryland yield by 18–25% via a single well-timed protective irrigation and balanced micronutrient/NPK dosing.
9. **Limitations:** Narrowing exclusively to minor millets in the pitch might make some judges ask why major crops like Paddy or Maize are excluded (better to support both major crops AND dryland crops in one unified engine).
10. **Demonstration Potential:** Comparative optimization showing how 1 protective irrigation of 30 mm at pegging/flowering in groundnut or sorghum protects 450 kg/ha of yield.

### Concept 7: BorewellCommons — Village-Level Groundwater Budget & Cooperative Crop-Mix Portfolio Optimizer
1. **Project Identity:**
   - **Name:** *BorewellCommons*
   - **One-Line Description:** A watershed-scale multi-agent crop-allocation and yield optimizer that helps a village or FPO choose a Rabi crop mix matching shared aquifer recharge.
   - **Main Objective:** Prevent tragedy-of-the-commons borewell failure in Rabi by optimizing village-wide acreage across high-water (paddy) vs. low-water, high-margin crops (maize, groundnut, chickpea).
   - **Primary Target User:** Gram Panchayats, Water User Associations (WUAs), and FPOs in hard-rock aquifer regions (Telangana, Maharashtra, Karnataka).
2. **Real-World Problem:** When monsoon rainfall is moderate, every farmer in a village independently plants water-intensive Rabi paddy; by February, the shared hard-rock aquifer collapses, causing 40–60% of tail-end fields to wither completely.
3. **Proposed Solution & User Journey:** Integrates monsoon cumulative recharge ([IMD](https://imdlib.readthedocs.io/en/latest/)/[NASA POWER](https://packages.ropensci.org/nasapower)), Sentinel-2 village water-body surface area, and crop water requirements ([FAO-56](https://www.fao.org/3/i2800e/i2800e.pdf)) to solve a village-level linear program recommending optimal crop-mix percentages and individual field switches.
4. **Innovation:** Shifts optimization from an isolated plot to a shared micro-watershed aquifer constraint.
5. **AI Involvement:** Satellite surface-water segmentation (NDWI) + multi-crop yield prediction + Markowitz-style portfolio linear programming.
6. **Data Requirements:** [CGWB / India-WRIS groundwater data](https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=2297646&reg=3&lang=1), Sentinel-2 NDWI, [NASA POWER](https://packages.ropensci.org/nasapower), and mandi price series.
7. **Technical Feasibility (15 Days):** Moderate; aquifer hydrogeology is difficult to calibrate at village scale without local piezometer logs.
8. **User Benefits:** Prevents late-season Rabi crop failure across entire village clusters.
9. **Limitations:** Collective action problem—individual farmers rarely switch crops solely for village-wide aquifer sustainability unless incentivized.
10. **Demonstration Potential:** Interactive watershed simulator showing village borewell depletion curves under 100% Paddy vs. an optimized 40% Paddy / 35% Maize / 25% Groundnut portfolio.

### Concept 8: PayoutGuard — Gram-Panchayat vs. Plot-Level Yield Basis-Risk Auditor & Stress Mitigation Trigger
1. **Project Identity:**
   - **Name:** *PayoutGuard*
   - **One-Line Description:** A satellite basis-risk detector that alerts farmers when their plot suffers localized stress invisible to Gram-Panchayat-level PMFBY insurance averages, triggering immediate agronomic mitigation or 72-hour claim documentation.
   - **Main Objective:** Protect smallholders from uninsured localized crop losses through early salvage interventions or automated geo-evidential claim filing.
   - **Primary Target User:** PMFBY-insured farmers and rural credit cooperatives.
2. **Real-World Problem:** [YES-TECH](https://www.pmfby.gov.in/pdf/YES_TECH_Manual_2023.pdf) estimates yield at Gram Panchayat scale; localized hailstorm, waterlogging, or pest pockets ruin individual farms without triggering panchayat-wide payouts, and farmers miss the 72-hour localized loss intimation rule.
3. **Proposed Solution & User Journey:** Continuously computes the z-score divergence between a farmer's 10m Sentinel-1/2 pixel cluster and the surrounding Gram Panchayat distribution. When divergence exceeds $-2.0\sigma$, it estimates whether the yield loss is recoverable (prescribing foliar rescue nutrition/drainage) or non-recoverable (auto-generating a timestamped satellite evidence packet for PMFBY localized claim filing).
4. **Innovation:** Directly complements India's national [YES-TECH framework](https://www.pmfby.gov.in/pdf/YES_TECH_Manual_2023.pdf) by solving plot-vs-unit basis risk.
5. **AI Involvement:** Spatiotemporal anomaly detection on Sentinel-1 SAR + Sentinel-2 optical series and yield loss attribution.
6. **Data Requirements:** [Sentinel-1/2](https://dataspace.copernicus.eu/), [NASA POWER](https://packages.ropensci.org/nasapower), and village boundary polygons.
7. **Technical Feasibility (15 Days):** Moderate-High, though focused heavily on insurance claim workflows rather than the hackathon prompt's core "reduce resource waste" mandate.
8. **User Benefits:** Recovers either salvageable yield or insurance compensation during localized weather shocks.
9. **Limitations:** Less focused on routine water and fertilizer waste reduction compared to Concepts 1, 2, and 3.
10. **Demonstration Potential:** Map view highlighting a flooded/stressed 2-hectare plot inside a healthy Gram Panchayat with one-click rescue vs. claim analysis.

